  "model": "s2.1-pro-free",
  "text": "After I left, Rina used the dry flour to make her dough. She shaped the loaves and baked the bread she had promised. I had been thinking about the gap in her roof. Rina had been thinking about the people coming for their bread. Replacing that tile had prevented the flour from getting wet, and she could get on with her work.\n\n“After I left, Rina used the dry flour to bake the bread she’d promised. You stayed with her, Pip. Later, you came with her to my workshop, and she brought me a loaf to say thank you. My page stops before any of that. I could write about the bread, or about her visit. I haven’t quite found the words for that part yet.”\n\nLater, Rina came to my workshop with Pip. She brought a loaf of bread and put it into my hands. I had thought it was only a small repair. She had taken time away from her bakery to thank me for it. For once, I set my tools aside before I had finished arranging them.\n\nFrom the ladder I found the cracked tile. The crack was narrow, but rain had a way through it. Rina had an intact spare. With her permission, Pip brought it from the shelf and carried it over to me. He held it with both hands until I had a firm grip.\n\nRain dripped through the roof of Rina’s bakery. I could hear it before I reached the door: a steady tap, a pause, another tap. Rina was dragging her flour sacks away from the puddles. She had moved one, but the water was spreading toward the next.\n\nI lifted out the damaged tile and set it aside. Then I fitted the spare into the opening. I eased it into place and checked the edges. From below, Rina asked whether it would hold. I gave it one last careful look before climbing down.",
